EXT. RAILROAD WATER TOWER - HIGH PLAINS - DAWN

A rusted tower on spindly legs. Dust devils on the flats. A single telegraph wire hums in the wind.

MARSHAL EZEKIEL DUNMORE, 50s, hat low, rides in on a gray mare and dismounts at the base. Waiting there: ODESSA PRATT-ADEYEMI, 30s, canvas duster, rifle across her lap. Beside her, a boy, LAZARUS, 14, holds a canteen and keeps his eyes on his boots.

Dunmore ties his horse. Takes his time.

DUNMORE
You sent the telegram.

ODESSA
I did.

DUNMORE
Said you had the Whitaker brothers.

ODESSA
I did, until they climbed the tower.

Dunmore looks up. Two silhouettes against the pale sky, cross-legged on the catwalk.

DUNMORE
They armed?

ODESSA
They've got a Winchester and a jug.

The telegraph wire sings. Dunmore pulls a pair of spectacles from his pocket, polishes them, puts them on. Lazarus finally looks up at him.

LAZARUS
Marshal. Are you going to shoot them?

DUNMORE
Not unless they ask nicely.

Lazarus almost smiles. Odessa lifts her rifle a hair.

RADIO (V.O.)
(crackling from the saddlebag)
Dunmore, this is Fort Bell. Report.

Dunmore pulls a small field radio from the saddle. Raises it to his mouth.

DUNMORE
Three at the tower. Two up high. Holding.

RADIO (V.O.)
Copy. Reinforcements by noon.

DUNMORE
They'll be too late.

He clicks it off. Takes the rifle from Odessa's lap, hands it back to her, butt first. Walks to the ladder. Puts his boot on the first rung.

ODESSA
Marshal.

He pauses. She meets his eyes. Neither speaks. Lazarus watches both of them. Dunmore tips his hat and climbs.
